{"config":{"lang":["en"],"separator":"[\\s\\-]+","pipeline":["stopWordFilter"],"fields":{"title":{"boost":1000.0},"text":{"boost":1.0},"tags":{"boost":1000000.0}}},"docs":[{"location":"","title":"Getting Started","text":"<p>Crystal language bindings for Nuklear</p>"},{"location":"#introduction","title":"Introduction","text":"<p>Nuklear-cr provides a lightweight immediate-mode GUI for use with the following backends:</p> <ul> <li>SDL3-Renderer</li> <li>SDL3-OpenGL3</li> </ul> <p>An immediate-mode GUI means that it does not hold the state of its widgets. Instead,  you call the functions for the elements you want to draw, and they are drawn every frame.</p> Example <pre><code># Outside the render loop\ncount = 0\n\n# Inside the render loop\nnk.window(\"demo\") do\n    nk.row\n    count += 1 if nk.button(\"Increase\")\n    nk.label \"Count: \" + count.to_s\nend\n</code></pre>  nk.button returns true if the button was clicked during the current frame. Since everything is redrawn every frame,  there is no need to worry about updating the value of a given label instance."},{"location":"#installation","title":"Installation","text":"<p>Work in progress</p>"},{"location":"#api-reference","title":"API Reference","text":""},{"location":"#Nuklear","title":"class <code>Nuklear</code> inherits <code>Reference</code>","text":"<p>Test</p>"},{"location":"#Nuklear-methods","title":"Methods","text":""},{"location":"#Nuklear#button(text)","title":"<code>#button(text)</code>","text":"View source"},{"location":"#Nuklear#chart_column(samples,min,max)","title":"<code>#chart_column(samples, min = 0, max = 100</code>","text":"View source"},{"location":"#Nuklear#chart_lines(samples,min,max)","title":"<code>#chart_lines(samples, min = 0, max = 100</code>","text":"View source"},{"location":"#Nuklear#chart_max(samples,min,max)","title":"<code>#chart_max(samples, min = 0, max = 100</code>","text":"View source"},{"location":"#Nuklear#chart_push_slot(val,index)","title":"<code>#chart_push_slot(val, 